nts. If any complement fails, reduce to that complement and restart with n=2. If no complement fails, increase n to min(2*n, len(current)). Here, \"double it up to current size\" means increase n (number of intervals) by doubling, up to the size of the current set (so n <= len(current)). So after no reduction, we double n (capped at len). That's standard.\n\nWe must also handle UNRESOLVED: only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. That means if a subset produces UNRESOLVED, we treat it like PASS? Or skip? The algorithm should test subsets, and if outcome is UNRESOLVED, we treat as not a failure, so we don't reduce to that subset, but we may still test complements? The description says \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOL"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":837,"completion_tokens":1024,"total_tokens":1861,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":512},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1024},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":512,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":325},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}